</style></head>
<body><h1>Checkout model experiment</h1><p>Disposable prototype · fictional basket · no real payments or inventory changes</p><p>Question: which failure requires compensation, and does payment occur before stock is guaranteed?</p>
<div aria-label="Simulated outcome"><button type="button" data-failure="none">Success</button> <button type="button" data-failure="inventory">Inventory failure</button> <button type="button" data-failure="payment">Payment failure</button></div>
<p id="scenario" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><div class="models"><article><h2>Model A: reserve, then pay</h2><p id="state-A"></p><ol id="trace-A"></ol></article><article><h2>Model B: pay, then reserve</h2><p id="state-B"></p><ol id="trace-B"></ol></article></div>
<p>Assumption: release and refund succeed immediately. Their delays and failures are not simulated.</p>
<script src="models.js"></script><script>
const names={none:'Success',inventory:'Inventory failure',payment:'Payment failure'};
function render(failure){
  for(const model of ['A','B']){
    const result=simulate(model,failure);
    document.getElementById('state-'+model).textContent='State: '+result.state+' | Inventory: '+(result.reserved?'reserved':'unreserved')+' | Payment: '+(result.charged?'charged':'not charged');
    const list=document.getElementById('trace-'+model);list.replaceChildren();
    for(const step of result.trace){const item=document.createElement('li');item.textContent=step;list.append(item);}
  }
  for(const button of document.querySelectorAll('button[data-failure]')) button.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(button.dataset.failure===failure));
  document.getElementById('scenario').textContent='Showing '+names[failure].toLowerCase()+' for both models.';
}
for(const button of document.querySelectorAll('button[data-failure]')) button.addEventListener('click',()=>render(button.dataset.failure));
render('none');
</script></body></html>
EOF
cat > experiment/README.txt <<'EOF'
Checkout state-model experiment
Open experiment/index.html in a browser, or serve this directory with python3 -m http.server.
Select Success, Inventory failure, or Payment failure. Both model traces update together.

Observation that settles the comparison: identify the compensation operation and whether a charge occurs before an inventory failure.
Observed via model harness:
- A + inventory failure: stops before payment; no compensation required.
- A + payment failure: inventory was held and must be released.
- B + payment failure: stops before reserving; no compensation required.
- B + inventory failure: payment was charged and must be refunded.
- Both success paths finish with reserved inventory and charged payment.

Carry forward explicit compensation states. A avoids charge/refund on stock failure; B avoids holding stock on payment failure. Selecting a production model still requires expected failure rates, inventory-hold constraints, and payment/refund guarantees. This experiment cannot select a winner from the supplied facts alone.

Assumptions: fictional one-item $25 basket, synchronous calls, release/refund always succeed. Compensation failures, delays, retries, concurrency, and real service integration are outside the experiment. Everything is in memory; production/ is untouched.
EOF
